// recomp_seeds.json — this game's recompiler seeds (external/psxport/tools/recomp/emit.py --seeds).
//
// The recompiler discovers functions from the binary itself: the entry point, the pointer/table scans,
// then the direct-`jal` call graph. What it CANNOT see is anything reached only through a function
// pointer, or entered at a runtime-computed re-entry point, or an overlay's load base. Those are facts
// about THIS executable and THIS disc, so the framework ships none of them and every game supplies its
// own file. JSON with `//` comments; every key is optional.
//
// The resident binary's entry point and binary scans provide 6,192 roots which grow to 7,533
// functions through direct-jal discovery. One measured runtime re-entry adds the 6,193rd seed and
// 7,534th emitted function:
// startIntr calls setjmp at 0x800E518C, and the live B0:0x19 HookEntryInt buffer holds 0x800E5194 as
// its saved return address. The BIOS exception exit resumes there with non-zero v0 so startIntr calls
// trapIntr. It belongs only in `main_reentry`: psxport's emitter now includes re-entry addresses in
// the seed union directly, so duplicating it in `main` would create two apparent authorities for one
// fact. tools/verify_cd_irq.py checks the retail edge and this exact wiring.
//
// Two rules, both learned the hard way elsewhere in this workspace:
//
//   * NEVER copy another game's seed list. A foreign seed that lands inside your text SPLITS a real
//     function at an arbitrary offset — the emit SUCCEEDS and the recomp is silently corrupt. This
//     repo has a TEMPTING supply of addresses in external/mmx4; an address taken from there is a
//     HYPOTHESIS until measured against these bytes, and it belongs here only with the disassembly line
//     that justifies it.
//   * NEVER guess an overlay base. An overlay is keyed BY its load address, so a wrong base emits a
//     whole module of correctly-decoded instructions at wrong addresses, and every jal target, pointer
//     test and router lookup is then silently wrong. A missing base is a HARD ERROR in emit.py,
//     deliberately — that refusal is the feature.
//
// Grow it empirically: boot, and when the substrate fail-fasts with `[recomp-MISS] 0x800xxxxx`, add
// that address WITH a note on how it is reached. An address with no rationale is unreviewable later.
//
// THIS GAME HAS NO CODE OVERLAYS, and that is MEASURED, not assumed: the boot executable is the whole
// engine (1,177,600 B of .text, 5,380 function epilogues), all 163 disc files were enumerated, all 138
// .ARC files read in full, and 0 of 138 contain a single `jr $ra`. Three independent signals agree and
// the decomp's splat config declares exactly one code segment with no overlays. See docs/codemap.md,
// docs/info/claims/ and `python3 tools/code_scan.py --census`. So `overlay_bases` /
// `overlay_base_patterns` / `overlay_seeds` are expected to stay EMPTY for this game — not "not yet
// filled". The stated blind spot: the method sees PLAIN R3000A code, so packed code inside an .ARC
// would be invisible to it. If a decompressor and a fixed load base ever turn up in the boot exe, that
// is the finding that reopens this.
//
// Before writing a word about this game's module shape, re-enumerate rather than trust this comment:
//     python3 tools/discdump.py list
//     PSXPORT_DIR=... python3 tools/code_scan.py --census scratch/raw/arc --glob '*.ARC' --ctrl-exe <exe>
{
  "main": [],
  "main_reentry": ["0x800E5194"],
  "overlay_bases": {},
  "overlay_base_patterns": [],
  "overlay_seeds": {}
}
